# 9. Final Model Results

The final reported results are approximately:

| Metric | Enhanced Model |
|---|---:|
| ROC-AUC | 0.7838 |
| PR-AUC | 0.0702 |
| Sensitivity | 65.6% |
| Specificity | 76.9% |
| Precision | 4.9% |
| Test recall improvement | About 10 percentage points |

Compared with the baseline:

- ROC-AUC improved from approximately 0.7598 to 0.7838.
- Sensitivity improved from approximately 55.25% to 65.6%.
- Test PR-AUC remained approximately similar to the baseline.

The honest conclusion is:

> The model clearly improves discrimination and recall, while PR-AUC remains approximately comparable to the baseline because the problem is highly imbalanced.

It is better to present this honestly rather than claiming improvement in every metric.

---

# 10. Probability Calibration

Raw machine-learning probabilities are not always reliable.

For example, a model output of `0.20` should ideally mean that similar cases have approximately a 20% event rate. Calibration makes the probabilities more meaningful.

The project compares:

- Platt scaling
- Isotonic calibration

Isotonic calibration was selected.

The calibration outputs include:

- Calibrated test probabilities
- Brier score
- Expected Calibration Error

The calibration error is approximately 0.0007–0.0009, indicating good agreement between predicted probabilities and observed outcomes.

---

# 11. Clinical Threshold Selection

The default classification threshold is not 50%.

The selected threshold is approximately:

```text
0.0262 = 2.62%
```

This may appear low, but sepsis is a rare event. A low threshold is reasonable when the goal is to identify more potentially septic patients.

The threshold was selected using the rule:

> Minimize the alarm rate while maintaining sensitivity of at least 60%.

At the selected threshold:

- Sensitivity is approximately 65.6%.
- Specificity is approximately 76.9%.
- Precision is approximately 4.9%.
- Alarm rate is approximately 23.9%.

This means the system prioritizes catching possible sepsis cases, accepting false alarms as a tradeoff.

You can explain this to your guide as:

> In an early-warning system, missing a patient who may develop sepsis can be more serious than generating additional alerts. Therefore, the threshold is selected around a sensitivity requirement rather than using the default 50% threshold.

